item.text:
Searches covered Vietnamese personnel records, fiscal resolutions, year-end disbursement reporting, World Bank assessment, PAPI citizen evidence, and PCI business evidence. The strongest additions are independently measured provincial implementation deterioration and mixed anti-corruption outcomes. The 2022 personnel meeting confirms collective appointment authority under Trọng’s chairmanship, but accessible records do not disclose selection deliberations or establish that he personally chose the replacements. Public-investment measures were followed by higher fiscal-close disbursement, but recovery-program components remained delayed and no source isolates Trọng’s causal contribution. Independent reporting about bureaucratic chilling was located, but deterministic access was blocked, so it is not entered below.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Party Central Committee's extraordinary meeting discusses personnel affairs","publisher":"VietnamPlus / Vietnam News Agency","publication_date":"2022-12-30","url":"https://en.vietnamplus.vn/party-central-committee-s-extraordinary-meeting-discusses-personnel-affairs/246386.vnp","claim":"At an extraordinary meeting on 30 December 2022 chaired by General Secretary Nguyễn Phú Trọng, the Party Central Committee released Phạm Bình Minh and Vũ Đức Đam from Central Committee positions and considered two candidates for the Politburo to nominate to the National Assembly as replacement deputy prime ministers. This establishes Trọng's chairing role and the collective Party nomination route, but the record gives no selection rationale, vote, or evidence that Trọng personally selected either candidate.","locator":"Opening paragraph and personnel-affairs paragraphs describing the 30 December extraordinary meeting, release decisions, and opinions on two deputy-prime-minister candidates; direct page retrieval was blocked after the indexed record was recovered","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-C1-001","canonical_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_deputy_pm_replacement_nomination_process","disposition":"discovery_only","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.4"],"source_type":"official_news_record","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Vietnam News Agency is authoritative for the formal meeting record, but deterministic page retrieval was blocked and the communiqué does not disclose deliberations or individual selection ownership.","final_evidence_use":"discovery_only","period_fit":"Directly concerns the 30 December 2022 personnel decision; appointments took effect in January 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Trọng chaired the collective Central Committee meeting; personal authorship of the candidate choices is not established.","contrary_evidence":"The documented procedure assigns decisions and nominations to collective Party organs followed by National Assembly approval, limiting sole-ruler attribution.","underlying_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_deputy_pm_replacement_nomination_process","lenses":["8B.4"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Appointment decisions announced for two Deputy Prime Ministers","publisher":"Government of Vietnam News Portal","publication_date":"2023-01-09","url":"https://baochinhphu.vn/cong-bo-quyet-dinh-bo-nhiem-hai-pho-thu-tuong-chinh-phu-102230109173031059.htm","claim":"The eventual replacements considered in December 2022 were Trần Hồng Hà and Trần Lưu Quang. The government described both as trained, politically experienced senior cadres: Hà had served as minister and deputy minister in the natural-resources portfolio, while Quang had served as Hải Phòng Party secretary and a National Assembly deputy. The formal appointment followed National Assembly approval on 5 January 2023. This supplies competence indicators but only an official, non-comparative rationale.","locator":"Paragraphs 189-203 and 223-225","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-C1-002","canonical_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_deputy_pm_replacement_candidate_qualifications","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.4"],"source_type":"official_appointment_record","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"The government record precisely identifies the appointees, procedure, and prior offices, but its competence assessment is promotional and the appointments occurred just after the target year.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Later source specifically identifying the candidates considered on 30 December 2022; formal appointments occurred 5 January 2023.","ruler_attribution":"No personal selection decision by Trọng is stated; appointments are attributed to Party, National Assembly, president, and prime-ministerial procedures.","contrary_evidence":"The formal process and the source's praise of the outgoing deputy prime ministers complicate any inference that replacement necessarily improved competence.","underlying_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_deputy_pm_replacement_nomination_process","lenses":["8B.4"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Resolution 124/NQ-CP on major tasks and solutions to promote public-investment disbursement in the remaining months of 2022","publisher":"Government of Vietnam Policy Portal","publication_date":"2022-09-16","url":"https://xaydungchinhsach.chinhphu.vn/toan-van-nghi-quyet-124-nq-cp-ve-cac-nhiem-vu-giai-phap-chu-yeu-thuc-day-giai-ngan-von-dau-tu-cong-nhung-thang-cuoi-nam-2022-119220916145907577.htm","claim":"After disbursement reached only 39.15% of the prime-ministerial plan by 31 August, the government ordered legal and administrative bottlenecks reviewed, assigned responsibility to agency and provincial heads, created enforcement accountability for delays, and targeted 95-100% general disbursement plus at least 50% of assigned recovery-program capital in 2022.","locator":"Resolution preamble and Sections I-II, paragraphs 85-100; especially paragraphs 86, 92-96","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-C1-003","canonical_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_resolution_124_disbursement_correction","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.8","8B.10"],"source_type":"official_resolution","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government resolution with precise baseline, targets, assigned responsibilities, and remedial measures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Issued and operative during September-December 2022.","ruler_attribution":"A government corrective action implementing Party and state priorities; the resolution does not show that Trọng designed or personally administered it.","contrary_evidence":"The resolution itself acknowledges that ten resolutions, four dispatches, seven directives, two conferences, and six working groups had not prevented low August execution.","underlying_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_public_investment_corrective_measures_and_execution","lenses":["8B.8","8B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Government’s regular meeting—October 2022","publisher":"Government of Vietnam News Portal","publication_date":"2023-01-31","url":"https://en.baochinhphu.vn/governments-regular-meeting-october-2022-11123013120195868.htm","claim":"By 31 October 2022, public-investment disbursement had risen to 51.34% of plan from 39.15% at the end of August, but remained below the comparable 2021 rate of 55.80%. The government therefore reiterated Resolution 124, leadership accountability, investment-procedure completion, and project-readiness measures. This shows some implementation movement after the September correction but not target attainment or a demonstrated causal effect.","locator":"Section III, paragraphs 305-313, especially paragraphs 306-311","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-C1-004","canonical_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_disbursement_october_progress","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.8","8B.10"],"source_type":"official_implementation_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Precise contemporaneous administrative figures tied to an identified Ministry of Planning and Investment report, though government-reported and not independently audited here.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Measures execution through 31 October 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Shared government implementation; no source evidence attributes the change specifically to Trọng.","contrary_evidence":"Execution remained behind 2021 and far below the 95-100% target, requiring repeated corrective instructions.","underlying_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_public_investment_corrective_measures_and_execution","lenses":["8B.8","8B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Public-investment disbursement and recovery-program implementation conference","publisher":"Vietnam Social Security, reproducing Vietnam News Agency reporting","publication_date":"2023-02-21","url":"https://vss.gov.vn/english/news/Pages/external-information.aspx?CateID=0&ItemID=10863","claim":"By the 31 January fiscal-execution cutoff, VND541.85 trillion of 2022 public-investment capital had been disbursed, equal to 93.5% of plan, with 30 localities and eight central bodies reaching 100%. However, the 2% recovery-program interest subsidy still lacked an effective mechanism for allocating VND2.8 trillion, and national-target-program disbursement reached only 57%. The corrective period therefore produced substantial aggregate execution but uneven program implementation.","locator":"Paragraphs 100-120, especially 106-119","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-C1-005","canonical_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_fiscal_close_disbursement_results","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.8","8B.10"],"source_type":"official_fiscal_close_report","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Provides precise Ministry of Planning and Investment figures and named lagging components, but is official reporting published after year-end and does not causally evaluate Resolution 124.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Later report specifically measuring execution of the 2022 capital plan through its January fiscal cutoff.","ruler_attribution":"Government-wide result principally discussed under Prime Minister Phạm Minh Chính; no personal operational role for Trọng is established.","contrary_evidence":"Aggregate disbursement improvement coexisted with slow interest-subsidy implementation and only 57% execution of national target programs.","underlying_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_public_investment_corrective_measures_and_execution","lenses":["8B.8","8B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Taking Stock: Vietnam Economic Update, March 2023","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2023-03-13","url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099544403132351453/pdf/idu0343e48530e212043860bee605aae66cfb04a.pdf","claim":"The World Bank attributed Vietnam’s 8% growth in 2022 mainly to post-COVID private-consumption recovery and export manufacturing, while the public sector contributed only about one percentage point because of public-investment implementation challenges. It further found that recovery-program investment projects were prepared during 2022 and expected only partial rollout from 2023 onward. Aggregate recovery therefore cannot be credited primarily to 2022 public-program execution.","locator":"PDF pages 10-12, lines 172-209 and 352-355; pages 35-38, lines 1566-1583 and 1645-1699","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-C1-006","canonical_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_recovery_shared_causes_and_public_investment_underexecution","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.7","8B.8","8B.10"],"source_type":"independent_economic_assessment","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent multilateral assessment using government fiscal and statistical data while explicitly separating growth contributors and implementation constraints.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published shortly afterward and specifically assesses 2022 growth and program execution.","ruler_attribution":"National outcomes are shared context; the report establishes neither personal ownership nor implementation by Trọng.","contrary_evidence":"Strong headline growth masks limited public-sector contribution and delayed recovery-program capital execution.","underlying_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_recovery_shared_causes_and_public_investment_underexecution","lenses":["8B.7","8B.8","8B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"The 2022 Viet Nam Provincial Governance and Public Administration Performance Index","publisher":"CECODES, VFF-CRT, RTA and UNDP","publication_date":"2023-04-12","url":"https://www.undp.org/sites/g/files/zskgke326/files/2024-01/2022_papi_report_eng_e-book.pdf","claim":"A nationally representative survey of 16,117 respondents found that local public-sector corruption control declined in 2022 across all four measured subdimensions—local-government corruption, public-service corruption, equity in state employment, and willingness to fight corruption. The share perceiving national corruption had decreased fell from 62.8% in 2021 to 60.67% in 2022, the first reversal in the series since 2018.","locator":"PDF executive summary pages 16-17, lines 584-610; Chapter 1 pages 37-40, lines 2282-2308 and 2583-2601","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-C1-007","canonical_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_papi_local_corruption_control_reversal","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.5","8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"],"source_type":"independent_citizen_survey","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Large nationwide survey measuring citizens’ direct experiences and perceptions across all provinces; it independently tests outcomes beyond official enforcement volume.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Survey measures governance and public-administration experience during calendar year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome evidence relevant to the system under Trọng, but it cannot isolate his personal causal contribution or the effect of committees formed partway through 2022.","contrary_evidence":"The measured deterioration conflicts with official claims that formation and enforcement volume alone demonstrated improved local anti-corruption functioning.","underlying_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_local_anticorruption_independent_outcomes","lenses":["8B.5","8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"The 2022 Viet Nam Provincial Governance and Public Administration Performance Index","publisher":"CECODES, VFF-CRT, RTA and UNDP","publication_date":"2023-04-12","url":"https://www.undp.org/sites/g/files/zskgke326/files/2024-01/2022_papi_report_eng_e-book.pdf","claim":"Despite deteriorating local measures, 80% of respondents believed the national government was serious about combating corruption in 2022—the highest level in the series—while confidence in provincial commitment declined from 69.07% in 2021 to 66.93%. This indicates strong national signaling but a central-local implementation gap.","locator":"PDF page 40-41, lines 2563-2631; Figure 1.10","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-C1-008","canonical_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_papi_national_local_anticorruption_gap","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.5","8B.6","8B.7"],"source_type":"independent_citizen_survey","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Large nationwide survey with directly reported national-versus-provincial comparisons; measures perceived commitment rather than objective corruption incidence.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct measurement of 2022 perceptions.","ruler_attribution":"High national confidence is consistent with visibility of the Trọng-chaired campaign, but does not prove effective provincial implementation.","contrary_evidence":"National seriousness reached a survey high while provincial confidence and experiential corruption-control measures declined.","underlying_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_local_anticorruption_independent_outcomes","lenses":["8B.5","8B.6","8B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"The Provincial Competitiveness Index PCI 2022","publisher":"Vietnam Chamber of Commerce and Industry with USAID support","publication_date":"2023-04-11","url":"https://www.pcivietnam.vn/uploads/EN-Bao-cao-dai/2022_PCI-Report_Final.pdf","claim":"Business survey evidence found worsening subnational implementation quality in 2022: 45.2% of firms said good provincial policies were not properly implemented by departments, up from 31.9% in 2021, and 50.4% reported improper district-level implementation, up from 36%. This independently documents an implementation deficit beyond enforcement and committee-formation counts.","locator":"PDF pages 22-23, lines 518-528; page 69, lines 2667-2697 and Figure 1.19","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-C1-009","canonical_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_pci_subnational_policy_implementation_deterioration","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.5","8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"],"source_type":"independent_business_survey","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Major annual firm survey with explicit year-over-year measures of department and district implementation; not designed to isolate the anti-corruption committees’ effect.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Measures firms’ experiences during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"System-level outcome during Trọng’s rule; no personal causal attribution is supported.","contrary_evidence":"Provincial policy initiative did not reliably translate into departmental or district implementation, weighing against claims of uniformly effective administration.","underlying_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_subnational_implementation_quality","lenses":["8B.5","8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"The Provincial Competitiveness Index PCI 2022","publisher":"Vietnam Chamber of Commerce and Industry with USAID support","publication_date":"2023-04-11","url":"https://www.pcivietnam.vn/uploads/EN-Bao-cao-dai/2022_PCI-Report_Final.pdf","claim":"The business survey recorded mixed enforcement quality: firms reporting informal payments to inspectors fell from 20.9% in 2021 to 14% in 2022, and firms spending over 10% of income on informal charges fell to 3.8% from 9.1% in 2016. Yet 42.6% still reported informal charges, 54.5% reported payments to tax officials versus 33.8% in 2021, and 71.7% said officials commonly caused procedural difficulties versus 57.4%.","locator":"PDF pages 65-71, lines 2489-2494 and 2698-2794; Figures 1.20-1.21","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-C1-010","canonical_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_pci_mixed_corruption_enforcement_outcomes","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.5","8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"],"source_type":"independent_business_survey","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct firm-experience indicators provide independent outcome evidence, with both improvements and contrary measures reported in the same methodology.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly measures 2022 business experiences and compares them with 2021 and longer-term baselines.","ruler_attribution":"The trends are compatible with effects of a national anti-corruption campaign but cannot be causally assigned to Trọng or to the new provincial committees.","contrary_evidence":"Reduced inspector bribery coexisted with sharply higher reported tax-official payments, procedural obstruction, and widespread informal charges.","underlying_fact_key":"vietnam_2022_local_anticorruption_independent_outcomes","lenses":["8B.5","8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}
